{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KT79TpsAi4ZCg3fA6MyUCt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Closed-form site response: acceleration field and shear strains for a one-dimensional inhomogeneous stratum\n2\t\n3\t## SLOT 1: Closed-form analytical solutions for the seismic site acceleration response\n4\t\n5\t### Physical Model and Coordinate Convention\n6\t\n7\tA horizontally infinite, fine-grained soil stratum of thickness $H_s$ overlies a rigid or compliant rock half-space and is subjected to a prescribed horizontal acceleration time history $a_g(t)$ applied at the rock-soil interface. The coordinate $z$ is measured upward from the rock-soil interface, so $z = 0$ coincides with the base and $z = H_s$ with the free surface. The depth from the free surface is denoted $\\eta = H_s - z$. The soil is treated as a linear viscoelastic material with uniform mass density $\\rho$ and a depth-dependent small-strain shear modulus $G(z)$ whose maximum value $G_o \\equiv G(0)$ is attained at the base. The base shear wave velocity is $c_o = \\sqrt{G_o/\\rho}$; the base wavenumber at circular frequency $\\omega$ is $k_o = \\omega/c_o$.\n8\t\n9\t### Governing Wave Equation and Boundary Conditions\n10\t\n11\tOne-dimensional, vertically propagating horizontally polarized shear (SH) waves in the stratum satisfy the equilibrium equation for a horizontal soil slice [@Gazetas1982][@SchnabelEtAl1972]:\n12\t\n13\t$$\\rho\\,\\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial t^2} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}\\!\\left[G(z)\\,\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\right]$$\n14\t\n15\twhere $u(z,t)$ is the horizontal displacement. For time-harmonic excitation with $u(z,t) = U(z,\\omega)\\,e^{i\\omega t}$, the equation reduces to the second-order ODE [@Gazetas1982]:\n16\t\n17\t$$\\frac{d}{dz}\\!\\left[G(z)\\,\\frac{dU}{dz}\\right] + \\rho\\,\\omega^2\\,U = 0$$\n18\t\n19\tTwo boundary conditions close the problem. At the free surface ($z = H_s$, $\\eta = 0$), the shear stress vanishes:\n20\t\n21\t$$\\left.\\frac{dU}{dz}\\right|_{z=H_s} = 0$$\n22\t\n23\tAt the rock-soil interface ($z = 0$), the displacement matches the prescribed base excitation:\n24\t\n25\t$$U(0,\\,\\omega) = U_g(\\omega)$$\n26\t\n27\tWhen the underlying rock is treated as a compliant elastic half-space, the base condition is replaced by a radiation condition incorporating the impedance ratio $\\alpha_I = \\rho c_o/(\\rho_r c_r)$ between the stratum and the rock [@Gazetas1982][@DobrEtAl1976]. For profiles where $G(H_s) = 0$ (i.e., $m > 0$ in the power-law family), the modulus vanishes at the free surface and the zero-stress condition is satisfied automatically; the operative surface constraint becomes regularity of the solution, which excludes the singular Bessel-$Y$ component [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011].\n28\t\n29\t### Frequency-Domain Transfer Functions\n30\t\n31\tThe acceleration transfer function is defined as $H_a(z,\\omega) = A(z,\\omega)/A_g(\\omega)$, where $A(z,\\omega) = -\\omega^2 U(z,\\omega)$ is the harmonic acceleration amplitude at elevation $z$ and $A_g(\\omega) = -\\omega^2 U_g(\\omega)$ is the base input amplitude. Because the $-\\omega^2$ factors cancel identically, the acceleration and displacement transfer functions share the same spatial form: $H_a(z,\\omega) = U(z,\\omega)/U_g(\\omega)$ [@Gazetas1982].\n32\t\n33\t#### Uniform Shear Stiffness\n34\t\n35\tFor $G(z) = G_o$ (constant throughout), the governing ODE reduces to $d^2U/dz^2 + k^2 U = 0$ with $k = \\omega/c_s$ and $c_s = \\sqrt{G_o/\\rho}$. Applying zero stress at $z = H_s$ and prescribed displacement at $z = 0$ yields the closed-form transfer function [@DobrEtAl1976][@SchnabelEtAl1972]:\n36\t\n37\t$$H_a(z,\\omega) = \\frac{\\cos(k\\,\\eta)}{\\cos(k\\,H_s)}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n38\t\n39\tAt the free surface ($\\eta = 0$), this simplifies to the classical surface amplification function $H_a = 1/\\cos(kH_s)$. The denominator vanishes at the resonant wavenumbers $k_n H_s = (2n-1)\\pi/2$, corresponding to the natural frequencies and mode shapes [@DobrEtAl1976]:\n40\t\n41\t$$f_n = \\frac{(2n-1)\\,c_s}{4\\,H_s}, \\qquad \\phi_n(\\eta) = \\cos\\!\\left[\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi\\,\\eta}{2\\,H_s}\\right], \\qquad n = 1, 2, 3, \\ldots$$\n42\t\n43\tThe fundamental frequency $f_1 = c_s/(4H_s)$ corresponds to the quarter-wavelength resonance condition $H_s = \\lambda_1/4$.\n44\t\n45\tThe time-domain acceleration field is recovered by modal superposition. Expanding the response as $u(z,t) = \\sum_n \\phi_n(\\eta)\\,q_n(t)$, each generalized coordinate satisfies an SDOF equation of motion driven by the base acceleration [@SDEE2022]:\n46\t\n47\t$$\\ddot{q}_n + 2\\xi\\,\\omega_n\\,\\dot{q}_n + \\omega_n^2\\,q_n = -\\Gamma_n\\,a_g(t)$$\n48\t\n49\tThe modal participation factor for a deposit with uniform density is:\n50\t\n51\t$$\\Gamma_n = \\frac{\\int_0^{H_s} \\rho\\,\\phi_n\\,dz}{\\int_0^{H_s} \\rho\\,\\phi_n^2\\,dz} = \\frac{4\\,(-1)^{n+1}}{(2n-1)\\,\\pi}$$\n52\t\n53\tand the total acceleration at any elevation is $a(z,t) = \\sum_n \\phi_n(\\eta)\\,\\ddot{q}_n(t)$.\n54\t\n55\t#### Linear Shear Modulus Profile (Gibson Deposit)\n56\t\n57\tThe Gibson deposit assigns a shear modulus that increases linearly from zero at the free surface to $G_o$ at the base:\n58\t\n59\t$$G(z) = G_o\\,\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n60\t\n61\tSubstituting into the governing ODE and using $\\eta$ as the independent variable yields $\\eta\\,U'' + U' + k_o^2 H_s\\,U = 0$. The substitution $s = 2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta}$ transforms this into Bessel's equation of order zero. Regularity at the free surface ($\\eta \\to 0$, $G \\to 0$) excludes the $Y_0$ component, and the base boundary condition fixes the amplitude. The resulting transfer function is [@Gazetas1982][@DobrEtAl1976]:\n62\t\n63\t$$H_a(z,\\omega) = \\frac{J_0\\!\\left(2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta}\\right)}{J_0\\!\\left(2k_o\\,H_s\\right)}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n64\t\n65\tThe natural frequencies are governed by the zeros $j_{0,n}$ of $J_0$ ($j_{0,1} \\approx 2.405$, $j_{0,2} \\approx 5.520$, $j_{0,3} \\approx 8.654$):\n66\t\n67\t$$\\omega_n = \\frac{j_{0,n}\\,c_o}{2\\,H_s}, \\qquad \\phi_n(\\eta) = J_0\\!\\left(j_{0,n}\\sqrt{\\eta/H_s}\\right)$$\n68\t\n69\tThis case corresponds to $m = 1$ in the general power-law family below.\n70\t\n71\t#### General Power-Law Profile\n72\t\n73\tA power-law shear modulus profile\n74\t\n75\t$$G(z) = G_o\\!\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}\\right)^m = G_o\\!\\left(\\frac{H_s - z}{H_s}\\right)^m, \\qquad 0 < m < 2$$\n76\t\n77\tadmits a closed-form Bessel-function solution for any real exponent $m$ in the open interval $(0,2)$. The substitution [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n78\t\n79\t$$\\zeta(\\eta) = \\frac{2\\,k_o\\,H_s}{2-m}\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}\\right)^{(2-m)/2}$$\n80\t\n81\ttransforms the governing ODE into Bessel's equation of order $\\nu = (1-m)/(2-m)$. Excluding the singular $Y_\\nu$ component by the regularity condition at $\\eta \\to 0$ and enforcing the base boundary condition gives the acceleration transfer function [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n82\t\n83\t$$H_a(z,\\omega) = \\left(\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}\\right)^{(1-m)/2}\\frac{J_\\nu\\!\\left[\\zeta(\\eta)\\right]}{J_\\nu\\!\\left[\\zeta(H_s)\\right]}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z, \\quad \\nu = \\frac{1-m}{2-m}$$\n84\t\n85\twhere $J_\\nu$ denotes the Bessel function of the first kind of order $\\nu$. The natural frequencies are determined by the zeros $j_{\\nu,n}$ of $J_\\nu$:\n86\t\n87\t$$\\omega_n = \\frac{j_{\\nu,n}\\,(2-m)\\,c_o}{2\\,H_s}$$\n88\t\n89\twith corresponding mode shapes $\\phi_n(\\eta) = (\\eta/H_s)^{(1-m)/2}\\,J_\\nu[\\zeta_n(\\eta)]$, where $\\zeta_n$ uses $j_{\\nu,n}$ in place of $\\zeta(H_s)$. The formula is not applicable at $m = 0$, for which the zero-stress boundary condition must be applied explicitly at the non-singular free surface, yielding the trigonometric transfer function of the uniform stratum [@Gazetas1982]. At $m = 1$ ($\\nu = 0$), the general formula reduces identically to the Gibson transfer function.\n90\t\n91\t### Layered Stratum: Thomson-Haskell Transfer Matrix\n92\t\n93\tFor a stratum composed of $N$ horizontal sublayers, each homogeneous, the displacement and stress state at the bottom of any layer is related to the state at its top by the layer propagator matrix. For layer $j$ of thickness $h_j$, complex shear wave velocity $V_{sj}^*$, and wavenumber $k_j^* = \\omega/V_{sj}^*$, the propagator matrix is [@SDEE2022]:\n94\t\n95\t$$\\mathbf{P}_j = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(k_j^* h_j) & \\sin(k_j^* h_j)/(G_j^* k_j^*) \\\\ -G_j^* k_j^*\\,\\sin(k_j^* h_j) & \\cos(k_j^* h_j) \\end{pmatrix}$$\n96\t\n97\tThe global transfer is obtained by multiplying layer matrices: $\\mathbf{P} = \\mathbf{P}_1\\,\\mathbf{P}_2\\,\\cdots\\,\\mathbf{P}_N$. The surface-to-base acceleration transfer function follows from the $(1,1)$ element of $\\mathbf{P}$ combined with the free-surface and base boundary conditions. This approach constitutes the standard discretized implementation of the closed-form layer solutions and converges to the continuous analytical formulas as sublayer thickness approaches zero.\n98\t\n99\t### Material Damping and Equivalent-Linear Extension\n100\t\n101\tMaterial damping is incorporated by replacing the real shear modulus $G(z)$ with the complex modulus $G^*(z) = G(z)(1 + 2i\\xi)$, where $\\xi$ is the hysteretic damping ratio, assumed depth-uniform for simplicity. The wavenumber becomes complex, $k^* \\approx k(1-i\\xi)$ for $\\xi \\ll 1$, and all transfer functions remain valid under the substitution $k_o \\to k_o^*$. The resonant poles acquire finite imaginary parts, bounding amplification at resonance to values proportional to $1/(2\\xi)$ for small $\\xi$ [@SchnabelEtAl1972][@RovithisEtAl2011].\n102\t\n103\tThe equivalent-linear method extends this viscoelastic framework to approximate nonlinear soil behavior by iterating between the frequency-domain solution and strain-compatible values of $G$ and $\\xi$ obtained from modulus reduction and damping curves at each depth [@SchnabelEtAl1972]. The closed-form transfer functions apply without modification within each iteration, because the soil is treated as linear viscoelastic during that pass.\n104\t\n105\t### Key Controlling Parameters\n106\t\n107\tThe acceleration response $a(z,t)$ is governed by five primary parameter groups: (1) stratum thickness $H_s$, which sets the resonant frequency scale through $f_n \\propto c_o/H_s$; (2) base shear wave velocity $c_o = \\sqrt{G_o/\\rho}$ and mass density $\\rho$; (3) stiffness profile exponent $m$, which controls the Bessel order $\\nu = (1-m)/(2-m)$, the mode shapes, and the distribution of modal energy with depth; (4) hysteretic damping ratio $\\xi$, which governs peak amplification at resonance; and (5) base impedance ratio $\\alpha_I = \\rho c_o/(\\rho_r c_r)$, which determines the fraction of seismic energy reflected back into the stratum and modifies resonant amplitudes when the base is compliant [@Gazetas1982][@DobrEtAl1976].\n108\t\n109\t---\n110\t\n111\t## SLOT 2: Closed-form equations for seismic shear strains derived from the acceleration field\n112\t\n113\tThe shear strain field $\\gamma(z,t)$ is derived from the acceleration transfer functions established above by differentiation of the displacement field with respect to $z$, or equivalently by vertical integration of the equation of motion from the free surface. All notation -- $z$, $\\eta = H_s - z$, $G_o$, $c_o$, $k_o$, $\\nu$, $\\zeta(\\eta)$ -- carries over from Slot 1 without redefinition.\n114\t\n115\t### Kinematic Definition and Exact Integral Representation\n116\t\n117\tThe engineering shear strain at any elevation $z$ is defined by the horizontal displacement gradient with respect to the vertical coordinate:\n118\t\n119\t$$\\gamma(z,t) = \\frac{\\partial u(z,t)}{\\partial z}$$\n120\t\n121\tAn exact, model-independent relationship between $\\gamma(z,t)$ and the acceleration field is obtained by integrating the equation of motion vertically from elevation $z$ to the free surface $H_s$. Applying the zero-stress free-surface condition $\\tau(H_s,t) = 0$ yields [@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n122\t\n123\t$$\\gamma(z,t) = -\\frac{\\rho}{G(z)}\\int_z^{H_s} a(z',t)\\,dz'$$\n124\t\n125\tEquivalently, in terms of depth from the free surface $\\eta = H_s - z$:\n126\t\n127\t$$\\gamma(\\eta,t) = \\frac{\\rho}{G(\\eta)}\\int_0^{\\eta} a(\\eta',t)\\,d\\eta'$$\n128\t\n129\tMultiplying both sides by $G(z)$ gives the shear stress profile [@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n130\t\n131\t$$\\tau(z,t) = G(z)\\,\\gamma(z,t) = -\\rho\\int_z^{H_s} a(z',t)\\,dz'$$\n132\t\n133\tThe shear stress at any level is governed exclusively by the inertia of the overlying soil mass and is independent of the specific form of $G(z)$. This property is an exact consequence of the equation of motion and holds for any differentiable stiffness profile.\n134\t\n135\t### Shear Strain for the Uniform Stratum\n136\t\n137\tFor $G(z) = G_o$ (constant), the integral representation reduces immediately to:\n138\t\n139\t$$\\gamma(\\eta,t) = \\frac{1}{c_s^2}\\int_0^{\\eta} a(\\eta',t)\\,d\\eta', \\qquad c_s = \\sqrt{G_o/\\rho}$$\n140\t\n141\tThe equivalent frequency-domain expression is obtained by differentiating $U(z,\\omega) = U_g\\cos(k\\eta)/\\cos(kH_s)$ with respect to $z$ (noting $dU/dz = -dU/d\\eta$) [@DobrEtAl1976][@SchnabelEtAl1972]:\n142\t\n143\t$$\\Gamma(z,\\omega) = \\frac{dU}{dz} = \\frac{k\\,U_g\\,\\sin(k\\eta)}{\\cos(kH_s)}$$\n144\t\n145\tSubstituting $U_g = -A_g/\\omega^2$ and using $k/\\omega^2 = 1/(c_s\\omega)$, the shear strain transfer function becomes:\n146\t\n147\t$$H_\\gamma(z,\\omega) = \\frac{\\Gamma(z,\\omega)}{A_g(\\omega)} = -\\frac{\\sin(k\\,\\eta)}{c_s\\,\\omega\\,\\cos(k\\,H_s)}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n148\t\n149\tThe spatial distribution is governed by $\\sin(k\\eta)$, which is zero at the free surface ($\\eta = 0$) -- consistent with the zero-stress boundary condition -- and attains its maximum amplitude near the base at each resonant frequency. This distribution is 90 degrees out of phase with the acceleration mode shapes $\\cos(k_n\\eta)$: the $n$-th mode contributes maximum acceleration at the free surface but maximum strain nearer the base [@DobrEtAl1976].\n150\t\n151\tIn the time domain, differentiating the modal expansion $u(z,t) = \\sum_n \\phi_n(\\eta)\\,q_n(t)$ with $\\phi_n(\\eta) = \\cos(k_n\\eta)$ with respect to $z$:\n152\t\n153\t$$\\gamma(z,t) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} k_n\\,\\sin(k_n\\,\\eta)\\,q_n(t), \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n154\t\n155\twhere $k_n = (2n-1)\\pi/(2H_s)$ and $q_n(t)$ is the SDOF modal response defined in Slot 1. At the base ($\\eta = H_s$), $\\sin(k_n H_s) = \\pm 1$ and all modes contribute with maximum amplitude; at the free surface ($\\eta = 0$), all modal strain contributions vanish [@GazetasDakoulas1985].\n156\t\n157\t### Shear Strain for the Linear (Gibson) Profile\n158\t\n159\tFor the linear profile $G(z) = G_o\\,\\eta/H_s$ with $\\eta = H_s - z$, differentiating $U(\\eta,\\omega) = U_g\\,J_0(2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta})/J_0(2k_oH_s)$ with respect to $z$ and applying the Bessel identity $dJ_0(x)/dx = -J_1(x)$ together with the chain rule gives [@Gazetas1982]:\n160\t\n161\t$$\\Gamma(z,\\omega) = \\frac{dU}{dz} = U_g\\,k_o\\sqrt{\\frac{H_s}{\\eta}}\\,\\frac{J_1\\!\\left(2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta}\\right)}{J_0(2k_o H_s)}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n162\t\n163\tSubstituting $U_g = -A_g/\\omega^2$ and $k_o/\\omega^2 = 1/(c_o\\omega)$:\n164\t\n165\t$$H_\\gamma(z,\\omega) = -\\frac{1}{c_o\\,\\omega}\\sqrt{\\frac{H_s}{\\eta}}\\,\\frac{J_1\\!\\left(2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta}\\right)}{J_0(2k_o H_s)}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n166\t\n167\tThe factor $\\sqrt{H_s/\\eta}$ reflects a geometric amplification of strain as the free surface is approached ($\\eta \\to 0$). As $\\eta \\to 0$, the shear modulus $G \\to 0$ while the shear stress $\\tau = G\\gamma$ remains bounded (because $J_1(x) \\to x/2$ as $x \\to 0$, so the product $\\sqrt{H_s/\\eta}\\cdot J_1(2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\eta}) \\to k_o H_s$ -- finite); nevertheless, the strain itself diverges in the linearized model at the free surface for this profile.\n168\t\n169\t### Shear Strain for the General Power-Law Profile\n170\t\n171\tFor the power-law profile $G(z) = G_o(\\eta/H_s)^m$ with $0 < m < 2$ and $\\eta = H_s - z$, the displacement transfer function is given by the Bessel formula of Slot 1. Differentiating with respect to $z$ (equivalently $-d/d\\eta$) and applying the Bessel recurrence relation $J_\\nu'(\\zeta) = J_{\\nu-1}(\\zeta) - (\\nu/\\zeta)J_\\nu(\\zeta)$, the $J_\\nu$ term in the resulting expression cancels identically -- a consequence of the algebraic identity between the power prefactor exponent $\\alpha = (1-m)/2$ and the Bessel order $\\nu = (1-m)/(2-m)$ -- leaving a single Bessel term of order $\\nu - 1$ [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n172\t\n173\t$$H_\\gamma(z,\\omega) = \\frac{\\Gamma(z,\\omega)}{A_g(\\omega)} = \\frac{1}{c_o\\,\\omega}\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}\\right)^{(1-2m)/2}\\frac{J_{\\nu-1}\\!\\left[\\zeta(\\eta)\\right]}{J_\\nu\\!\\left[\\zeta(H_s)\\right]}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n174\t\n175\twhere\n176\t\n177\t$$\\zeta(\\eta) = \\frac{2\\,k_o\\,H_s}{2-m}\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}\\right)^{(2-m)/2}, \\qquad \\nu = \\frac{1-m}{2-m}, \\qquad k_o = \\frac{\\omega}{c_o}$$\n178\t\n179\tInternal consistency is confirmed at $m = 1$: the Bessel order reduces to $\\nu - 1 = -1$, $J_{-1} = -J_1$, $\\zeta(\\eta) = 2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta}$, and the prefactor becomes $\\sqrt{H_s/\\eta}$, reproducing the linear-profile formula with correct sign [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011].\n180\t\n181\tThe depth dependence of the strain amplitude is controlled by the power-law factor $(\\eta/H_s)^{(1-2m)/2}$. For $m > 1/2$, the exponent $(1-2m)/2 < 0$ and this factor amplifies toward the free surface ($\\eta \\to 0$), producing a near-surface strain concentration. For $m < 1/2$, the exponent is positive and the algebraic factor decays toward the surface; whether the net strain increases or decreases near the surface depends additionally on the frequency-dependent Bessel term.\n182\t\n183\t### Depth Distribution and Influence of the Stiffness Profile\n184\t\n185\tThe depth distribution of shear strains is shaped jointly by the stiffness profile $G(z)$ and the modal structure of the acceleration solution. For the uniform layer, the strain modes $\\sin(k_n\\eta)$ are 90 degrees out of phase with the acceleration modes $\\cos(k_n\\eta)$; the fundamental mode therefore contributes maximum strain nearer the base, while higher modes introduce additional zero-crossings at intermediate depths [@DobrEtAl1976].\n186\t\n187\tFor non-uniform profiles with $m > 0$, the algebraic prefactor $(\\eta/H_s)^{(1-2m)/2}$ modulates the Bessel contribution with depth. As $m$ increases toward 2, the stiffness contrast between base and surface grows and near-surface strain concentration intensifies. The shear stress $\\tau(z,t) = G(z)\\,\\gamma(z,t)$ remains bounded at the free surface for all $m < 2$, as required by the zero-stress boundary condition, even when the strain itself diverges algebraically for $m \\geq 1/2$ [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011].\n188\t\n189\tFor applications that require the strain field from a known acceleration profile, the integral form provides a direct computational path. For the uniform layer, this yields:\n190\t\n191\t$$\\gamma(\\eta,t) = \\frac{1}{c_s^2}\\int_0^\\eta a(\\eta',t)\\,d\\eta'$$\n192\t\n193\twhich expresses the shear strain as a depth-integrated measure of the acceleration field normalized by the square of the elastic wave velocity, directly applicable when the acceleration record is available from site response analysis or measurement [@SimCenterTFT2023][@GazetasDakoulas1985].\n194\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d1daeeb0-6096-4b8d-a82e-0a7bcbad6e4b","uuid":"7a9d758a-c098-44da-a02f-f64309e1f5b3","timestamp":"2026-03-29T19:15:34.964Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/poreB/kb/models.md","content":"# Closed-form site response: acceleration field and shear strains for a one-dimensional inhomogeneous stratum\n\n## SLOT 1: Closed-form analytical solutions for the seismic site acceleration response\n\n### Physical Model and Coordinate Convention\n\nA horizontally infinite, fine-grained soil stratum of thickness $H_s$ overlies a rigid or compliant rock half-space and is subjected to a prescribed horizontal acceleration time history $a_g(t)$ applied at the rock-soil interface. The coordinate $z$ is measured upward from the rock-soil interface, so $z = 0$ coincides with the base and $z = H_s$ with the free surface. The depth from the free surface is denoted $\\eta = H_s - z$. The soil is treated as a linear viscoelastic material with uniform mass density $\\rho$ and a depth-dependent small-strain shear modulus $G(z)$ whose maximum value $G_o \\equiv G(0)$ is attained at the base. The base shear wave velocity is $c_o = \\sqrt{G_o/\\rho}$; the base wavenumber at circular frequency $\\omega$ is $k_o = \\omega/c_o$.\n\n### Governing Wave Equation and Boundary Conditions\n\nOne-dimensional, vertically propagating horizontally polarized shear (SH) waves in the stratum satisfy the equilibrium equation for a horizontal soil slice [@Gazetas1982][@SchnabelEtAl1972]:\n\n$$\\rho\\,\\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial t^2} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}\\!\\left[G(z)\\,\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\right]$$\n\nwhere $u(z,t)$ is the horizontal displacement. For time-harmonic excitation with $u(z,t) = U(z,\\omega)\\,e^{i\\omega t}$, the equation reduces to the second-order ODE [@Gazetas1982]:\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dz}\\!\\left[G(z)\\,\\frac{dU}{dz}\\right] + \\rho\\,\\omega^2\\,U = 0$$\n\nTwo boundary conditions close the problem. At the free surface ($z = H_s$, $\\eta = 0$), the shear stress vanishes:\n\n$$\\left.\\frac{dU}{dz}\\right|_{z=H_s} = 0$$\n\nAt the rock-soil interface ($z = 0$), the displacement matches the prescribed base excitation:\n\n$$U(0,\\,\\omega) = U_g(\\omega)$$\n\nWhen the underlying rock is treated as a compliant elastic half-space, the base condition is replaced by a radiation condition incorporating the impedance ratio $\\alpha_I = \\rho c_o/(\\rho_r c_r)$ between the stratum and the rock [@Gazetas1982][@DobrEtAl1976]. For profiles where $G(H_s) = 0$ (i.e., $m > 0$ in the power-law family), the modulus vanishes at the free surface and the zero-stress condition is satisfied automatically; the operative surface constraint becomes regularity of the solution, which excludes the singular Bessel-$Y$ component [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011].\n\n### Frequency-Domain Transfer Functions\n\nThe acceleration transfer function is defined as $H_a(z,\\omega) = A(z,\\omega)/A_g(\\omega)$, where $A(z,\\omega) = -\\omega^2 U(z,\\omega)$ is the harmonic acceleration amplitude at elevation $z$ and $A_g(\\omega) = -\\omega^2 U_g(\\omega)$ is the base input amplitude. Because the $-\\omega^2$ factors cancel identically, the acceleration and displacement transfer functions share the same spatial form: $H_a(z,\\omega) = U(z,\\omega)/U_g(\\omega)$ [@Gazetas1982].\n\n#### Uniform Shear Stiffness\n\nFor $G(z) = G_o$ (constant throughout), the governing ODE reduces to $d^2U/dz^2 + k^2 U = 0$ with $k = \\omega/c_s$ and $c_s = \\sqrt{G_o/\\rho}$. Applying zero stress at $z = H_s$ and prescribed displacement at $z = 0$ yields the closed-form transfer function [@DobrEtAl1976][@SchnabelEtAl1972]:\n\n$$H_a(z,\\omega) = \\frac{\\cos(k\\,\\eta)}{\\cos(k\\,H_s)}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n\nAt the free surface ($\\eta = 0$), this simplifies to the classical surface amplification function $H_a = 1/\\cos(kH_s)$. The denominator vanishes at the resonant wavenumbers $k_n H_s = (2n-1)\\pi/2$, corresponding to the natural frequencies and mode shapes [@DobrEtAl1976]:\n\n$$f_n = \\frac{(2n-1)\\,c_s}{4\\,H_s}, \\qquad \\phi_n(\\eta) = \\cos\\!\\left[\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi\\,\\eta}{2\\,H_s}\\right], \\qquad n = 1, 2, 3, \\ldots$$\n\nThe fundamental frequency $f_1 = c_s/(4H_s)$ corresponds to the quarter-wavelength resonance condition $H_s = \\lambda_1/4$.\n\nThe time-domain acceleration field is recovered by modal superposition. Expanding the response as $u(z,t) = \\sum_n \\phi_n(\\eta)\\,q_n(t)$, each generalized coordinate satisfies an SDOF equation of motion driven by the base acceleration [@SDEE2022]:\n\n$$\\ddot{q}_n + 2\\xi\\,\\omega_n\\,\\dot{q}_n + \\omega_n^2\\,q_n = -\\Gamma_n\\,a_g(t)$$\n\nThe modal participation factor for a deposit with uniform density is:\n\n$$\\Gamma_n = \\frac{\\int_0^{H_s} \\rho\\,\\phi_n\\,dz}{\\int_0^{H_s} \\rho\\,\\phi_n^2\\,dz} = \\frac{4\\,(-1)^{n+1}}{(2n-1)\\,\\pi}$$\n\nand the total acceleration at any elevation is $a(z,t) = \\sum_n \\phi_n(\\eta)\\,\\ddot{q}_n(t)$.\n\n#### Linear Shear Modulus Profile (Gibson Deposit)\n\nThe Gibson deposit assigns a shear modulus that increases linearly from zero at the free surface to $G_o$ at the base:\n\n$$G(z) = G_o\\,\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n\nSubstituting into the governing ODE and using $\\eta$ as the independent variable yields $\\eta\\,U'' + U' + k_o^2 H_s\\,U = 0$. The substitution $s = 2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta}$ transforms this into Bessel's equation of order zero. Regularity at the free surface ($\\eta \\to 0$, $G \\to 0$) excludes the $Y_0$ component, and the base boundary condition fixes the amplitude. The resulting transfer function is [@Gazetas1982][@DobrEtAl1976]:\n\n$$H_a(z,\\omega) = \\frac{J_0\\!\\left(2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta}\\right)}{J_0\\!\\left(2k_o\\,H_s\\right)}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n\nThe natural frequencies are governed by the zeros $j_{0,n}$ of $J_0$ ($j_{0,1} \\approx 2.405$, $j_{0,2} \\approx 5.520$, $j_{0,3} \\approx 8.654$):\n\n$$\\omega_n = \\frac{j_{0,n}\\,c_o}{2\\,H_s}, \\qquad \\phi_n(\\eta) = J_0\\!\\left(j_{0,n}\\sqrt{\\eta/H_s}\\right)$$\n\nThis case corresponds to $m = 1$ in the general power-law family below.\n\n#### General Power-Law Profile\n\nA power-law shear modulus profile\n\n$$G(z) = G_o\\!\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}\\right)^m = G_o\\!\\left(\\frac{H_s - z}{H_s}\\right)^m, \\qquad 0 < m < 2$$\n\nadmits a closed-form Bessel-function solution for any real exponent $m$ in the open interval $(0,2)$. The substitution [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n\n$$\\zeta(\\eta) = \\frac{2\\,k_o\\,H_s}{2-m}\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}\\right)^{(2-m)/2}$$\n\ntransforms the governing ODE into Bessel's equation of order $\\nu = (1-m)/(2-m)$. Excluding the singular $Y_\\nu$ component by the regularity condition at $\\eta \\to 0$ and enforcing the base boundary condition gives the acceleration transfer function [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n\n$$H_a(z,\\omega) = \\left(\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}\\right)^{(1-m)/2}\\frac{J_\\nu\\!\\left[\\zeta(\\eta)\\right]}{J_\\nu\\!\\left[\\zeta(H_s)\\right]}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z, \\quad \\nu = \\frac{1-m}{2-m}$$\n\nwhere $J_\\nu$ denotes the Bessel function of the first kind of order $\\nu$. The natural frequencies are determined by the zeros $j_{\\nu,n}$ of $J_\\nu$:\n\n$$\\omega_n = \\frac{j_{\\nu,n}\\,(2-m)\\,c_o}{2\\,H_s}$$\n\nwith corresponding mode shapes $\\phi_n(\\eta) = (\\eta/H_s)^{(1-m)/2}\\,J_\\nu[\\zeta_n(\\eta)]$, where $\\zeta_n$ uses $j_{\\nu,n}$ in place of $\\zeta(H_s)$. The formula is not applicable at $m = 0$, for which the zero-stress boundary condition must be applied explicitly at the non-singular free surface, yielding the trigonometric transfer function of the uniform stratum [@Gazetas1982]. At $m = 1$ ($\\nu = 0$), the general formula reduces identically to the Gibson transfer function.\n\n### Layered Stratum: Thomson-Haskell Transfer Matrix\n\nFor a stratum composed of $N$ horizontal sublayers, each homogeneous, the displacement and stress state at the bottom of any layer is related to the state at its top by the layer propagator matrix. For layer $j$ of thickness $h_j$, complex shear wave velocity $V_{sj}^*$, and wavenumber $k_j^* = \\omega/V_{sj}^*$, the propagator matrix is [@SDEE2022]:\n\n$$\\mathbf{P}_j = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(k_j^* h_j) & \\sin(k_j^* h_j)/(G_j^* k_j^*) \\\\ -G_j^* k_j^*\\,\\sin(k_j^* h_j) & \\cos(k_j^* h_j) \\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nThe global transfer is obtained by multiplying layer matrices: $\\mathbf{P} = \\mathbf{P}_1\\,\\mathbf{P}_2\\,\\cdots\\,\\mathbf{P}_N$. The surface-to-base acceleration transfer function follows from the $(1,1)$ element of $\\mathbf{P}$ combined with the free-surface and base boundary conditions. This approach constitutes the standard discretized implementation of the closed-form layer solutions and converges to the continuous analytical formulas as sublayer thickness approaches zero.\n\n### Material Damping and Equivalent-Linear Extension\n\nMaterial damping is incorporated by replacing the real shear modulus $G(z)$ with the complex modulus $G^*(z) = G(z)(1 + 2i\\xi)$, where $\\xi$ is the hysteretic damping ratio, assumed depth-uniform for simplicity. The wavenumber becomes complex, $k^* \\approx k(1-i\\xi)$ for $\\xi \\ll 1$, and all transfer functions remain valid under the substitution $k_o \\to k_o^*$. The resonant poles acquire finite imaginary parts, bounding amplification at resonance to values proportional to $1/(2\\xi)$ for small $\\xi$ [@SchnabelEtAl1972][@RovithisEtAl2011].\n\nThe equivalent-linear method extends this viscoelastic framework to approximate nonlinear soil behavior by iterating between the frequency-domain solution and strain-compatible values of $G$ and $\\xi$ obtained from modulus reduction and damping curves at each depth [@SchnabelEtAl1972]. The closed-form transfer functions apply without modification within each iteration, because the soil is treated as linear viscoelastic during that pass.\n\n### Key Controlling Parameters\n\nThe acceleration response $a(z,t)$ is governed by five primary parameter groups: (1) stratum thickness $H_s$, which sets the resonant frequency scale through $f_n \\propto c_o/H_s$; (2) base shear wave velocity $c_o = \\sqrt{G_o/\\rho}$ and mass density $\\rho$; (3) stiffness profile exponent $m$, which controls the Bessel order $\\nu = (1-m)/(2-m)$, the mode shapes, and the distribution of modal energy with depth; (4) hysteretic damping ratio $\\xi$, which governs peak amplification at resonance; and (5) base impedance ratio $\\alpha_I = \\rho c_o/(\\rho_r c_r)$, which determines the fraction of seismic energy reflected back into the stratum and modifies resonant amplitudes when the base is compliant [@Gazetas1982][@DobrEtAl1976].\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 2: Closed-form equations for seismic shear strains derived from the acceleration field\n\nThe shear strain field $\\gamma(z,t)$ is derived from the acceleration transfer functions established above by differentiation of the displacement field with respect to $z$, or equivalently by vertical integration of the equation of motion from the free surface. All notation -- $z$, $\\eta = H_s - z$, $G_o$, $c_o$, $k_o$, $\\nu$, $\\zeta(\\eta)$ -- carries over from Slot 1 without redefinition.\n\n### Kinematic Definition and Exact Integral Representation\n\nThe engineering shear strain at any elevation $z$ is defined by the horizontal displacement gradient with respect to the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\\gamma(z,t) = \\frac{\\partial u(z,t)}{\\partial z}$$\n\nAn exact, model-independent relationship between $\\gamma(z,t)$ and the acceleration field is obtained by integrating the equation of motion vertically from elevation $z$ to the free surface $H_s$. Applying the zero-stress free-surface condition $\\tau(H_s,t) = 0$ yields [@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n\n$$\\gamma(z,t) = -\\frac{\\rho}{G(z)}\\int_z^{H_s} a(z',t)\\,dz'$$\n\nEquivalently, in terms of depth from the free surface $\\eta = H_s - z$:\n\n$$\\gamma(\\eta,t) = \\frac{\\rho}{G(\\eta)}\\int_0^{\\eta} a(\\eta',t)\\,d\\eta'$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $G(z)$ gives the shear stress profile [@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n\n$$\\tau(z,t) = G(z)\\,\\gamma(z,t) = -\\rho\\int_z^{H_s} a(z',t)\\,dz'$$\n\nThe shear stress at any level is governed exclusively by the inertia of the overlying soil mass and is independent of the specific form of $G(z)$. This property is an exact consequence of the equation of motion and holds for any differentiable stiffness profile.\n\n### Shear Strain for the Uniform Stratum\n\nFor $G(z) = G_o$ (constant), the integral representation reduces immediately to:\n\n$$\\gamma(\\eta,t) = \\frac{1}{c_s^2}\\int_0^{\\eta} a(\\eta',t)\\,d\\eta', \\qquad c_s = \\sqrt{G_o/\\rho}$$\n\nThe equivalent frequency-domain expression is obtained by differentiating $U(z,\\omega) = U_g\\cos(k\\eta)/\\cos(kH_s)$ with respect to $z$ (noting $dU/dz = -dU/d\\eta$) [@DobrEtAl1976][@SchnabelEtAl1972]:\n\n$$\\Gamma(z,\\omega) = \\frac{dU}{dz} = \\frac{k\\,U_g\\,\\sin(k\\eta)}{\\cos(kH_s)}$$\n\nSubstituting $U_g = -A_g/\\omega^2$ and using $k/\\omega^2 = 1/(c_s\\omega)$, the shear strain transfer function becomes:\n\n$$H_\\gamma(z,\\omega) = \\frac{\\Gamma(z,\\omega)}{A_g(\\omega)} = -\\frac{\\sin(k\\,\\eta)}{c_s\\,\\omega\\,\\cos(k\\,H_s)}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n\nThe spatial distribution is governed by $\\sin(k\\eta)$, which is zero at the free surface ($\\eta = 0$) -- consistent with the zero-stress boundary condition -- and attains its maximum amplitude near the base at each resonant frequency. This distribution is 90 degrees out of phase with the acceleration mode shapes $\\cos(k_n\\eta)$: the $n$-th mode contributes maximum acceleration at the free surface but maximum strain nearer the base [@DobrEtAl1976].\n\nIn the time domain, differentiating the modal expansion $u(z,t) = \\sum_n \\phi_n(\\eta)\\,q_n(t)$ with $\\phi_n(\\eta) = \\cos(k_n\\eta)$ with respect to $z$:\n\n$$\\gamma(z,t) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} k_n\\,\\sin(k_n\\,\\eta)\\,q_n(t), \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n\nwhere $k_n = (2n-1)\\pi/(2H_s)$ and $q_n(t)$ is the SDOF modal response defined in Slot 1. At the base ($\\eta = H_s$), $\\sin(k_n H_s) = \\pm 1$ and all modes contribute with maximum amplitude; at the free surface ($\\eta = 0$), all modal strain contributions vanish [@GazetasDakoulas1985].\n\n### Shear Strain for the Linear (Gibson) Profile\n\nFor the linear profile $G(z) = G_o\\,\\eta/H_s$ with $\\eta = H_s - z$, differentiating $U(\\eta,\\omega) = U_g\\,J_0(2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta})/J_0(2k_oH_s)$ with respect to $z$ and applying the Bessel identity $dJ_0(x)/dx = -J_1(x)$ together with the chain rule gives [@Gazetas1982]:\n\n$$\\Gamma(z,\\omega) = \\frac{dU}{dz} = U_g\\,k_o\\sqrt{\\frac{H_s}{\\eta}}\\,\\frac{J_1\\!\\left(2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta}\\right)}{J_0(2k_o H_s)}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n\nSubstituting $U_g = -A_g/\\omega^2$ and $k_o/\\omega^2 = 1/(c_o\\omega)$:\n\n$$H_\\gamma(z,\\omega) = -\\frac{1}{c_o\\,\\omega}\\sqrt{\\frac{H_s}{\\eta}}\\,\\frac{J_1\\!\\left(2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta}\\right)}{J_0(2k_o H_s)}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n\nThe factor $\\sqrt{H_s/\\eta}$ reflects a geometric amplification of strain as the free surface is approached ($\\eta \\to 0$). As $\\eta \\to 0$, the shear modulus $G \\to 0$ while the shear stress $\\tau = G\\gamma$ remains bounded (because $J_1(x) \\to x/2$ as $x \\to 0$, so the product $\\sqrt{H_s/\\eta}\\cdot J_1(2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\eta}) \\to k_o H_s$ -- finite); nevertheless, the strain itself diverges in the linearized model at the free surface for this profile.\n\n### Shear Strain for the General Power-Law Profile\n\nFor the power-law profile $G(z) = G_o(\\eta/H_s)^m$ with $0 < m < 2$ and $\\eta = H_s - z$, the displacement transfer function is given by the Bessel formula of Slot 1. Differentiating with respect to $z$ (equivalently $-d/d\\eta$) and applying the Bessel recurrence relation $J_\\nu'(\\zeta) = J_{\\nu-1}(\\zeta) - (\\nu/\\zeta)J_\\nu(\\zeta)$, the $J_\\nu$ term in the resulting expression cancels identically -- a consequence of the algebraic identity between the power prefactor exponent $\\alpha = (1-m)/2$ and the Bessel order $\\nu = (1-m)/(2-m)$ -- leaving a single Bessel term of order $\\nu - 1$ [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n\n$$H_\\gamma(z,\\omega) = \\frac{\\Gamma(z,\\omega)}{A_g(\\omega)} = \\frac{1}{c_o\\,\\omega}\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}\\right)^{(1-2m)/2}\\frac{J_{\\nu-1}\\!\\left[\\zeta(\\eta)\\right]}{J_\\nu\\!\\left[\\zeta(H_s)\\right]}, \\qquad \\eta = H_s - z$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\\zeta(\\eta) = \\frac{2\\,k_o\\,H_s}{2-m}\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{H_s}\\right)^{(2-m)/2}, \\qquad \\nu = \\frac{1-m}{2-m}, \\qquad k_o = \\frac{\\omega}{c_o}$$\n\nInternal consistency is confirmed at $m = 1$: the Bessel order reduces to $\\nu - 1 = -1$, $J_{-1} = -J_1$, $\\zeta(\\eta) = 2k_o\\sqrt{H_s\\,\\eta}$, and the prefactor becomes $\\sqrt{H_s/\\eta}$, reproducing the linear-profile formula with correct sign [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011].\n\nThe depth dependence of the strain amplitude is controlled by the power-law factor $(\\eta/H_s)^{(1-2m)/2}$. For $m > 1/2$, the exponent $(1-2m)/2 < 0$ and this factor amplifies toward the free surface ($\\eta \\to 0$), producing a near-surface strain concentration. For $m < 1/2$, the exponent is positive and the algebraic factor decays toward the surface; whether the net strain increases or decreases near the surface depends additionally on the frequency-dependent Bessel term.\n\n### Depth Distribution and Influence of the Stiffness Profile\n\nThe depth distribution of shear strains is shaped jointly by the stiffness profile $G(z)$ and the modal structure of the acceleration solution. For the uniform layer, the strain modes $\\sin(k_n\\eta)$ are 90 degrees out of phase with the acceleration modes $\\cos(k_n\\eta)$; the fundamental mode therefore contributes maximum strain nearer the base, while higher modes introduce additional zero-crossings at intermediate depths [@DobrEtAl1976].\n\nFor non-uniform profiles with $m > 0$, the algebraic prefactor $(\\eta/H_s)^{(1-2m)/2}$ modulates the Bessel contribution with depth. As $m$ increases toward 2, the stiffness contrast between base and surface grows and near-surface strain concentration intensifies. The shear stress $\\tau(z,t) = G(z)\\,\\gamma(z,t)$ remains bounded at the free surface for all $m < 2$, as required by the zero-stress boundary condition, even when the strain itself diverges algebraically for $m \\geq 1/2$ [@Gazetas1982][@RovithisEtAl2011].\n\nFor applications that require the strain field from a known acceleration profile, the integral form provides a direct computational path. For the uniform layer, this yields:\n\n$$\\gamma(\\eta,t) = \\frac{1}{c_s^2}\\int_0^\\eta a(\\eta',t)\\,d\\eta'$$\n\nwhich expresses the shear strain as a depth-integrated measure of the acceleration field normalized by the square of the elastic wave velocity, directly applicable when the acceleration record is available from site response analysis or measurement [@SimCenterTFT2023][@GazetasDakoulas1985].\n","numLines":194,"startLine":1,"totalLines":194}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01AcG7p275hdHCe1CBp3jLYD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me analyze the task:\n\n**Task (SLOT 1):** Implement an R algorithm that:\n- Takes a base acceleration time history $a_g(t)$ as input\n- Estimates the instantaneous shear strain history $\\gamma(z, t)$ at any depth within the stratum\n- Uses the analytical site response formulations from the KB\n- Uses the closed-form solutions to compute acceleration response at arbitrary depths\n- Then obtains shear strain histories from those accelerations\n\n**Key constraints:**\n- R code must use `data.table()` for data manipulation\n\n\n- Naming conventions should follow camelCase or dot notation rather than snake_case\n- Professional engineering methodology in English\n- Deliverable is a single slot document\n\nFrom the knowledge base, I'm working with two primary computational approaches. For uniform strata, I'll use modal superposition with SDOF responses, where the mode shapes follow a cosine pattern, natural frequencies scale with the shear wave velocity, and each mode has a participation factor of 4.\n\nNow I'm setting up the equations of motion for each mode under ground acceleration input, then reconstructing the acceleration field by summing the modal contributions. For power-law velocity profiles, I'm switching to the frequency domain using Bessel functions to define the transfer function, then transforming back to the time domain via inverse FFT.\n\nFor the shear strain calculation, I need to integrate the acceleration field vertically—either from the surface down or from the base up depending on the formulation—using the relationship between strain and the integral of acceleration weighted by the inverse shear modulus. The implementation will compute the acceleration at multiple depths using these analytical solutions, then apply the integral formula to get the strain field at each depth and time step. For the naming convention, I should stick with camelCase since that's standard in R. Now I'm structuring the manuscript to include an introduction, the mathematical foundation referencing the knowledge base, the R implementation, and a clear algorithm description. The code needs to handle the base acceleration input along with soil parameters, compute depth-dependent accelerations using frequency-domain transfer functions, and then vertically integrate to get the shear strain history.\n\nWriting R implementation... I'm setting up the FFT of the base acceleration and constructing the frequency vector, then adjusting for the circular convention where frequencies above the Nyquist limit wrap around as negative frequencies.\n\nNow I'm computing the complex wavenumber vector by accounting for the frequency-dependent behavior with damping, using the relationship between angular frequency and the complex shear wave velocity.\n\nFor the zero frequency case, I'm setting the wavenumber to zero since static loading produces no dynamic response. Then I'm defining a helper function that computes the transfer function for a power-law velocity profile—handling the uniform case separately with a simple cosine solution, and using Bessel functions for the general power-law case.\n\nNow I'm setting up the vertical integration grid and preparing to compute strain at each depth.\n\nComputing the shear modulus at each grid point using a power-law relationship with depth, then looping through each target depth to calculate the acceleration time series. Still writing the frequency loop... Completing the trapezoidal integration to get the strain coefficient, then applying an inverse FFT to convert back to the time domain and packaging the results with their corresponding depth and eta values. I'm noticing there might be some issues with this computational approach that I should reconsider.\n\nThe nested loops over frequencies and integration points are creating a bottleneck, and I'm calling `sapply` repeatedly which adds overhead. A vectorized approach would be much more efficient—computing the transfer function values across all integration depths at once for each frequency rather than looping through them individually.\n\nActually, stepping back, I need to reconsider the overall structure. The task describes deriving the strain field from the acceleration field using analytical site response formulations, which means I should first compute the full acceleration field at multiple depths using transfer functions, then integrate vertically to get the strain. Let me restructure the algorithm to follow this two-step process more clearly.\n\nWriting the acceleration field computation function... Still writing the loop...\n\nNow I'm stepping back to reconsider the overall approach. The core task is to take an input acceleration time history and compute the shear strain at different depths, which requires computing the acceleration field first using frequency domain methods, then deriving strain from that.\n\nThe implementation needs to use data.table and camelCase naming, with helper functions for the transfer function and the main algorithm that loops through depths to build up the acceleration field before computing the strain history. I'm realizing that R's `besselJ` function doesn't handle complex arguments directly, which is a problem since the damping is represented through a complex wavenumber. I have a few options here: I could use a specialized package like `Bessel` that supports complex values, approximate with real computation and corrections, or switch to a time-domain numerical approach like Newmark integration. Given the knowledge base describes modal superposition in detail for the uniform case, I'll focus on implementing that with numerical integration for each mode.\n\nFor the general case with power-law variation, I'll use a frequency-domain approach with FFT and transfer functions, though I'll need to work around the complex Bessel limitation by using real-valued approximations for lightly damped systems. Actually, the cleanest solution is to implement time-domain modal superposition across all cases, which avoids the complex Bessel issue entirely. (if mode shapes and frequencies are available)\n2. Use Duhamel's integral or Newmark integration for each SDOF\n\nFor the general power-law case, the mode shapes and natural frequencies involve Bessel functions but are real-valued. The damping is handled via the SDOF equations.\n\nFrom KB: the mode shapes for general power-law are $\\phi_n(\\eta) = (\\eta/H_s)^{(1-m)/2} J_\\nu[\\zeta_n(\\eta)]$ where $\\zeta_n$ uses $j_{\\nu,n}$ (zeros of $J_\\nu$) in place of $\\zeta(H_s)$.\n\nThe modal participation factors would need to be computed as integrals. For uniform strata, the participation factors are analytically known as $\\Gamma_n = 4(-1)^{n+1}/((2n-1)\\pi)$, but for power-law profiles I'd need to evaluate them numerically using the integral definition. A two-pass algorithm seems most practical: first compute the acceleration response in the frequency domain using FFT and transfer functions at depth nodes, then integrate vertically at each time step.\n\nFor damping in the frequency domain, I'm working with complex wavenumbers where the complex shear modulus introduces an imaginary component—for small damping ratios, the complex wavenumber becomes approximately $k(1-i\\xi)$. I can use R's Bessel function capabilities to handle the complex arguments needed for this calculation.\n\nFor a practical implementation, I'm shifting to a modal superposition approach with Duhamel integration. For the uniform case, I'm setting up the mode shapes as cosines with specific wavenumbers, calculating the natural frequencies and modal participation factors, then formulating each mode as a damped SDOF system driven by the ground acceleration, which I'll solve using Newmark time integration.\n\nNow for the power-law case with variable stiffness, I need to work out the mode shapes more carefully—they involve Bessel functions with a scaling that depends on the power-law exponent, and I'm double-checking the exact form from the reference to make sure I have the argument and normalization right. Now I'm deriving the natural frequencies by setting the Bessel function argument equal to its zeros, which gives me $\\omega_n = \\frac{j_{\\nu,n}(2-m)c_o}{2H_s}$. With this, I can construct the mode shape as a Bessel function with the normalized radial coordinate raised to a power, and I'm moving on to work out the modal participation factor and effective mass.\n\nFor the implementation strategy, I'm planning to use a frequency-domain approach with FFT and analytical transfer functions as the main method, then verify it against modal superposition for the uniform case. I'll leverage R's built-in Bessel function for the undamped calculations and handle damping through complex wavenumbers in the transfer function approach. I'm realizing the modal superposition approach is more practical for this problem—it avoids needing complex Bessel functions and works well for the general case. I'll find the zeros of the Bessel function, compute the mode shapes and participation factors numerically, then solve the damped equations for each mode using Newmark integration and sum the contributions.\n\nWriting the R implementation... I'm handling two cases for computing natural frequencies and mode shapes: for uniform strata I'm using analytical solutions with cosine mode shapes, but for power-law velocity profiles I need to work with Bessel functions and find their zeros numerically. Computing modal participation factors...\n\nNow I'm setting up the Newmark time integration scheme with constant average acceleration parameters to solve the single-degree-of-freedom equation for each mode, where the modal response is driven by the ground acceleration scaled by the participation factor.\n\nSetting up the time stepping loop... Building the acceleration field...\n\nNow I'm computing the shear strain by integrating the acceleration field vertically, accounting for how the shear modulus varies with depth depending on whether we're using a constant velocity model or a power-law gradient. Still writing integration logic...\n\nNow I'm moving on to a helper function that finds the zeros of Bessel functions—it searches through a range of x values to locate sign changes in the Bessel function output, then uses those sign changes to pinpoint the exact zeros.\n\nI'm also implementing a simple trapezoidal integration function to handle numerical integration.\n\nLooking at the overall implementation, I'm noticing a few potential issues: the Newmark-beta time integration scheme needs verification, and there's a structural problem where the `modeShape` function is being defined conditionally inside if blocks rather than once at the top level.\n\nI should also reconsider the naming conventions—the code currently uses camelCase, which aligns better with the constraint against snake_case, though I could explore dot.notation as an alternative if needed.\n\nFor the Newmark method itself, I'm working through the formulation for a damped oscillator. The effective stiffness calculation involves the natural frequency ω, damping ratio ξ, and the Newmark parameters β and γ, but I need to verify the complete expression since it appears to be cut off mid-formula. I'm setting up the standard Newmark method properly—solving for the acceleration at the next timestep using the equations of motion and the kinematic relations that express displacement and velocity in terms of accelerations. I'm switching to a displacement-based formulation instead, where I solve for the incremental displacement using an effective stiffness and effective force that accounts for the mass and damping contributions from the previous step. Actually, let me simplify this further by working with the normalized equation of motion directly. Now I'm completing the predictor step for velocity, then setting up the corrector equation where I solve for the acceleration at the next time step by substituting the predicted values into the equation of motion and isolating the acceleration term. I'm reconsidering whether a frequency-domain approach using FFT might be simpler and more standard for earthquake engineering problems. The method would involve transforming the base acceleration to the frequency domain, applying a transfer function at each frequency component, then transforming back to get the response. For damping, I'd use a complex wave velocity that incorporates the damping ratio.\n\nThe uniform case has a clean analytical transfer function, but the power-law case requires Bessel functions with complex arguments, which is where things get complicated. I think the practical solution is to implement the frequency-domain method for uniform layers and use modal superposition for the general power-law case, since both approaches should converge anyway.\n\nFor the actual implementation, I'll go with modal superposition—it's self-contained and only needs base R plus `data.table`, which is cleaner for a research paper than introducing a dependency on the `Bessel` package.\n\nWriting the implementation... Now I'm closing out the Bessel zero-finding function and moving into a new function that computes natural frequencies, mode shapes, and modal participation factors based on the water depth, wave speed, and number of modes requested. For a uniform stratum, I can derive these analytically using wavenumber formulas.\n\nContinuing with the power-law profile case...\n\nNow I'm computing the participation factors numerically using quadrature integration. I'm evaluating the mode shapes at discrete points along the depth, then calculating the normalized participation factor for each mode by taking the ratio of the integrated mode shape to the integrated squared mode shape.\n\nAfter that, I'm setting up the Newmark